module Incomplete.Gu09 where

{- INFERENCE GIVES UP WHERE CHECKING SUCCEEDS -- the refactor that does not
   help.

   THE SCENARIO.  A user who has hit Gu03 does the obvious thing: the definition
   is too big, so split it.  Four dimensions make a "core" order-line view; the
   other four are the finance-side attributes bolted on afterwards.  This is
   good practice independent of any compiler -- `coreOrderLines` is a useful
   thing to have by name, since half the reports only need that much.

       coreOrderLines orderLine productDim regionDim channelDim calendarDim
       wideOrderLines ... = coreOrderLines ... ** customerDim ** ...

   WHAT A COMPETENT USER EXPECTS.  Each half is a four-dimension star join, and
   Gu01's ladder puts a four-dimension join at 0.14s.  Two of those plus one
   application should be well under a second.  Splitting a definition in two is
   the standard way to make a type checker's life easier.

   WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS.  It does not come back -- see the family notes for the
   measurement.  Gu10 is these SAME TWO DEFINITIONS with the call removed, and
   it compiles in under half a second.  So neither half is expensive.  Composing
   them is.

   THE INCOMPLETENESS.  The blow-up is not a property of a long expression that
   could be broken up; it is a property of the constraint system, and naming an
   intermediate does not shrink it.  `coreOrderLines` generalises to a scheme of
   twelve partition constraints over nine row variables, and the caller
   instantiates all twelve at once and then adds twelve more -- the same system
   Gu03 has, arrived at by a different route.  The user's instinct (break it up)
   and the compiler's actual requirement (write the constraints down, Gu04) are
   different actions, and nothing in the error -- there is no error -- tells
   them which they need.
-}

import Prelude
import Relation
import Syntax.Relation

-- The order-line view most reports need.
coreOrderLines orderLine productDim regionDim channelDim calendarDim =
  orderLine ** productDim ** regionDim ** channelDim ** calendarDim

-- ...and the finance attributes the quarterly pack adds on top.
wideOrderLines orderLine productDim regionDim channelDim calendarDim
               customerDim currencyDim taxDim promoDim =
  coreOrderLines orderLine productDim regionDim channelDim calendarDim
    ** customerDim ** currencyDim ** taxDim ** promoDim
